## Mini‑Reference: Functions to Use (and When)

| Function | When to use | Why it matters |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| `operator[]` | Insert *or* default‑construct a value if missing. | Use only if you really want insertion. |
| `at()` | Retrieve a value with bounds‑checking. | Throws `std::out_of_range` if key missing. |
| `find()` | Lookup without insertion. | O(log n) for map, O(1) average for unordered_map. |
| `contains()` (C++20) | Check existence without needing the iterator. | Clear intent, no copy. |
| `count()` | For multimap to count duplicates. | Returns number of elements with the key. |
| `insert()` | Insert a new element; fails if key already present. | Returns pair<iterator,bool>. |
| `emplace()` | Construct element in place; still constructs value even if key exists. | Fast for unique insertion, but can waste work. |
| `try_emplace()` | Construct element only if key missing. | Avoids duplicate construction; best for maps. |
| `reserve()` / `rehash()` | Pre‑allocate buckets for unordered_map. | Prevents rehashes during bulk inserts. |
| `max_load_factor()` | Tune when rehash occurs. | Lower load factor => more buckets => less collisions. |
| `extract()` / `node_handle` | Move an element out of a container without copying. | Useful for LRU caches, re‑insertion. |
| `merge()` (C++17) | Move all elements from one associative container to another. | Efficient bulk move. |
| `erase(iterator)` / `erase(key)` | Remove elements; be careful of iterator validity. | `erase(iterator)` returns next iterator. |

